WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.253
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.253 --> 00:00:10.760
I'm really excited to
welcome Genie Yoo here today,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.750
because her work on
colonial era natural history

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.570
in the Dutch East Indies promises
to resonate with scholars

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.290
across Yale who are
interested in Southeast Asia,

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:25.520
the environmental humanities,
human nature interactions,

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.340
science and technology studies,
colonial history and more.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.750
Genie Yoo is a PhD candidate

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.370
in history at Princeton University

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.020
and currently a short-term fellow

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.610
at the John Carter Brown Library,

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.410
you can see from the background,

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.003
that library in the background.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.240
She's trained in early
modern and modern history

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.460
of Island Southeast Asia,
and works at the intersection

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.080
of history of science,
medicine, religious studies,

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.910
and the history of the book.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:58.500
She's completing her
dissertation, "Mediating Islands,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.390
Ambon Across the Ages"
under the supervision

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.000
of Michael Laffan and Keith Wailoo.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.240
And she's also the author
of a very powerful article

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.887
called "Wars and Wonders:

00:01:08.887 --> 00:01:11.267
"The Inter-Island Information Networks

00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:14.270
"of Georg Eberhard Rumphius"

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.600
published in the British Journal
for the History of Science,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.680
as well as numerous fascinating

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.443
and well-written book reviews.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.870
Please join me in welcoming
Genie Yoo for today's talk,

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:29.767
which is entitled, "Paper
Chains from Fields to Archives:

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:33.597
A View of the Information
Economy in 18th Century

00:01:33.597 --> 00:01:35.850
Colonial Indonesia."

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.683
Genie.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.749
- Thank you so much, Erik,

00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:42.100
and thank you to everyone
who's attending today

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.420
and to the council on Southeast
Asian studies at Yale,

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.840
and of course, to Kris Mooseker
who helped to organize this

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:52.983
since the summer.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.910
Today, I'll be presenting on a small part

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:02.070
of the fourth chapter for my dissertation,

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:06.450
which explores how cross-cultural
exchange shaped European

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.100
and indigenous knowledge formations

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.750
about unbalance natural
world from the 17th

00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:13.601
to the 20th century.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.460
And what I'll be presenting
today takes us back

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.460
to the 18th century.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.180
Let me share my...

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.870
So Paper Chains from Fields to Archives:

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.270
A View of the Local Information Economy

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.470
in the 18th Century Colonial Indonesia.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.710
Assam Soliman was not a
man of quiet disposition,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.083
or so the documents would have us believe.

00:02:38.485 --> 00:02:41.260
Soliman's voice captured in translation

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.870
came roaring across the
pages of company records,

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.763
copied again and again
in the 18th century.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:53.090
The year was 1705, a clear
day in the month of May,

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.770
the beginning of the
year's drawing season.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.650
Captain Lieutenant De
Bevere who had traveled

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.920
to the province of Ambon
for a council meeting

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.360
had no idea that what
he was about to witness

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.210
would make any kind of impact

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.423
on future Dutch East India
Company considerations.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.040
According to De Bevere,
an uncommon commotion

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.840
marked what was supposed to
be a routine council meeting

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.863
in one of the Island's
most lucrative provinces.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.490
Assam Soliman cause
high status at Orankaya

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.770
guaranteed them a seat at the council

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:33.133
had protested vociferously
against new company demands.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.330
The company was demanding
that administrators

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.863
lower the production of
cloves on the island.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.780
This was partly a response
to an intractable problem

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.560
rankling the Dutch East India
Company's high government

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.410
in Batavia.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.820
Millions of pounds of
stored cloves were rotting

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:56.120
and administrators could
neither sell them for profit

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.790
nor leave them to decay
in the humid spaces

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.013
of the city's cramped warehouses.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.260
One of the ways to keep
this problem at bay,

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:08.960
at least momentarily, was
to reduce the total number

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.750
of fresh cloves shipped from Ambon,

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:15.790
where they were mainly
produced to Batavia and Java,

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.683
the center of VOC operations
in the East Indies.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.160
Soliman was flabbergasted
in hearing the demand

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.100
so counterintuitive to
company expectations

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.990
for the past century, during
which time the islands people

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:32.370
had become economically dependent

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.723
on the company's commerce and cloves.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.340
The Ambonese, Soliman emphasized
had labored tirelessly

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.760
for years to meet the
demands of the company

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.760
despite their "suffering and
neglect of their own trees."

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.663
But now the company
wanted the very opposite.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.517
Exasperated, Soliman
was to have explained,

00:04:56.517 --> 00:04:59.337
"Are we not now poor enough?

00:04:59.337 --> 00:05:02.877
"What is so hard about letting
them know of our condition?

00:05:02.877 --> 00:05:06.887
"Just let them load the ships
with ropes to strangle us,

00:05:06.887 --> 00:05:09.317
"then we shall deal with
more of these cloves

00:05:09.317 --> 00:05:10.357
"and then some."

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:16.510
The islands administrators
impeded in significance

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.073
to Soliman's retort.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:23.440
On the last day of May,
1705, the governor of Ambon,

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.410
a man named Balthasar Coijet
sent a letter marked secret

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.663
to administrators in Batavia.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.620
In it he described me as he
had heard from De Beveres

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:39.620
who supposedly repeated
Soliman word for word in Dutch.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:43.360
Now it is impossible to know
if this was what Soliman

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.763
had actually said at the council meeting.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.290
If he was indeed protesting the decrease

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:52.230
in the production of
cloves, why would he say,

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:55.713
then we shall deal with more
of these cloves and then some.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.330
Well there's reasons for doubt, Coijet

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.200
exceeded confidence in its fidelity.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.570
He mentioned that De Bevere who witnessed

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:09.240
and belayed at this
incident would soon testify

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.150
to its veracity in person
by "word of mouth,"

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:15.983
since he was already on
a ship bound for Batavia.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.890
While this may sound somewhat dramatic,

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:24.400
this was relatively
speaking in minor event

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.693
in the history of Ambon.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:31.900
However, it has an important
bureaucratic afterlife

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.563
in the VOC provincial archive in Batavia.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:39.830
The contents of Coijet's
letter would be extracted

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:44.180
and copied in company reports,
demonstrations, resolutions,

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:45.763
and considerations.

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:50.210
This was in part a coincidence,
but it also happened

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.590
at a time when the company
was honing a complex system

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:56.500
of information collection and retrieval

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.950
through their active use of past documents

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:00.903
from provincial archives.

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:06.800
Just four years after the
incident was recorded,

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.610
a man by the name of Joan Van Hoorn,

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.100
a prominent administrator in Batavia

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.060
who would soon become the
VOC's governor general

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:19.820
extracted Soliman's retort
from Coijet's letter in full,

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:21.950
and included it in assigned demonstration

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:23.100
to the high government.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.330
This was only the beginning.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.500
A chain of paperwork would
carry Soliman's words

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:32.760
deep into the recesses of the
company's provincial archive

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.423
in Batavia, allowing them
to resurface intermittently

00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:38.940
throughout the century.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:41.700
Van Hoorn's lengthy
demonstration would be copied

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.500
word for word into the
book of general resolutions

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.260
for that year.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.160
This rendered the incident
and the coded traces

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.850
of Soliman's words we
treatable and extractable

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.460
through the use of what
would eventually be known

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.740
as the Realia Repertorium,

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:00.370
a working index of select marginalia

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:02.993
from the high government's
daily resolutions.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.720
Assam Soliman's name
would appear yet again

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.830
in the latter half of the 18th century.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:13.660
In 1775 report about
cultivating Bandas native

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:15.363
nutmeg trees in Ambon.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:21.280
So 70 years after Coijet related
in a secret letter in 1705,

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.180
Soliman's words would
be used in this report

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:29.163
but reinterpreted under vastly
different circumstances.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:34.090
Since the 16th century,
natural expeditions

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:35.590
were one of the main sources

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:39.290
of European information
gathering in the East Indies.

00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:42.810
Expensive, but valuable such expeditions

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:45.610
became a ubiquitous part
of imperial activities

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:47.710
in the early modern period.

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:50.120
Producing a wealth of books, descriptions,

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.380
drawings and paintings
about plants and animals,

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:56.543
minerals, and stones,
people their cultures.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:00.960
As many have argued,
they conveyed information

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.370
advantageous for global commerce,

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:06.983
significant for scholarship
and useful for empires.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:11.650
In this respect, the 18th
century was no different.

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:15.200
Marked as it was by
voyages global dimensions,

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:18.440
the most famous of which were
newly sponsored expeditions

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.910
for the gathering of natural historical,

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:24.630
as well as ethnographic
knowledge and specimens

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.173
for trade and for scholarship.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:31.163
This presentation asks a
slightly different question.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.330
After a century or more of collecting

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:35.983
and the Dutch East Indies,

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:40.280
how was past knowledge about
the natural world retrieved

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:42.270
and how was it read as information

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:46.653
having present economic value
pertinent to global commerce.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:50.250
While explorers and administrators

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.180
continue to undertake expeditions,

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:56.640
others began to rely on their
own provincial archives,

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.280
re-interpreting the past
and their present context

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.710
of the 18th century to
create considerations

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:05.373
pertinent to their governance
in the archipelago.

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.060
There are three main questions

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:12.330
I would like to address today,

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:16.360
inspired by this bureaucratic afterlife

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:19.310
of Assam Soliman's words.

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:24.310
First, what were the archival
strategies of VOC clerks

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:27.630
who retrieved information from the past?

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:31.513
In other words, how did
administrators read to govern?

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:36.540
Second and more specifically,
how did the active extraction

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:38.680
of past information from the archive

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.763
affect colonial governance?

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:46.350
And third, how might the
history of the VOC's active use

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:50.160
of material archives at the
site of colonial governance

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:52.620
help us to understand
their attempt to sustain

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:56.890
the political economy of the
VOC during its rapid decline

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:58.793
towards the end of the 18th century?

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:04.840
The answers to these questions
are I believe connected.

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:07.760
And I'm going to
demonstrate it in two parts.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:11.940
First, I'm going to show
how VOC clerks, marginalists

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:15.910
and archivists created an
actively used a new system

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:19.130
of information retrieval
in the 18th century,

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:23.123
a system which involved the
use of a massive working index.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:27.600
Then I will address how
retrieved information

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:32.260
from past documents were
integrated into new reports,

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:34.910
which influenced
administrative considerations

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:37.220
about labor in particular,

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:39.770
towards the end of the 18th century,

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:43.210
a time when the company was
completely drowning in debt,

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:46.850
no longer fruitfully
invested in inter-Asian trade

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.933
and crippled by inter
Imperial competition.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.400
VOC scribes understood that being able

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:01.530
to retrieve information

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:04.710
was just as important as collecting them.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.620
The retrieval of information
about a specific thing

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.030
from the company's vast archive

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.043
proved to be a formidable task.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.660
This was because the
company's daily log books

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:19.023
and resolutions have been
recorded and preserved by date.

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:23.760
Chronology in short, formed
the organizational backbone

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:26.540
of the archive, and written information

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:29.393
lived under the shadow of concrete dates.

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:33.520
And recognizing the need
for a more efficient system

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:37.530
of information retrieval,
administrators in the 18th century

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:41.790
began to create an alphabetical
index of specific things

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:46.403
and names like cloves and
letters, nutmeg and quicksilver.

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:49.650
Over the course of the century,

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:52.090
they compiled an overwhelming number

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:55.420
of paratextual materials about real things

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:58.640
and places from the
company's handwritten tones

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:00.343
of general resolutions.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.700
As the company's collecting
activities continued

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:06.580
and papers accumulated,

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:09.970
these indexes became a valuable tool,

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:13.660
a guide for reaching back,
not just into any past,

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:16.053
but the past of material things.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:19.810
The first page of these
handwritten indexes

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:23.800
still preserved in Jakarta,
or the title general register

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:27.630
of the order of the alphabet
in the order of the alphabet,

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:28.920
which in the 19th century,

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:31.650
it was printed as one master index

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:36.650
of three volumes enlightened,
titled Realia Repertorium.

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:41.010
In organizing a system
of information retrieval

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:44.310
by way of an index, clerks were beholden

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:45.920
to the extent marginalia

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:48.540
that summarized a much lengthier passages

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:51.230
in the body of the resolutions.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:54.210
Since each indexed item of
marginalia was accompanied

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:58.280
by a specific date, a clerk
could have easily found

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:01.800
the full resolution by
looking at the pertinent day

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:04.393
in the book of general
resolutions for that year.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:10.150
Towards the end of the 18th century,

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:14.920
most certainly in the year 1789 and 1790,

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.850
a group of clerks in
Batavia created a collection

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.930
of extracted passages from resolutions

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:24.203
dated between 1703 and 1719.

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:28.520
The collection concern
one matter in particular,

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:31.970
the prodigious rot of
millions of pounds of cloves

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:34.520
in Batavia's warehouses,

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.500
which was partly why Assam Soliman

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:39.320
was asked to lower the
production of cloves in Ambon

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:40.893
back in 1705.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.940
Behind this history of
how early 18th century

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:48.750
administrators manage
the city's vast amount

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:53.307
of decaying waste lies a
history of how late 18th century

00:14:53.307 --> 00:14:56.600
VOC clerks were able to
retrieve that information

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:58.853
from an equally vast amount of paper.

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:04.890
In this case, one can actually
retrace how clerks retrieve

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:07.480
the past of information from the index

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:10.870
and its list of dated
marginalia for cloves

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:13.530
to original counts from
the company's annual book

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:16.640
of general resolutions,
out of which they created

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:18.203
selected extracts.

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:22.900
The clerk's names signed on
every document they copied

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:25.160
or checked, where the only clues

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:27.300
to the time of its collection.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:30.060
In other words, the date of
the retrieval is not mentioned

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:32.800
in these papers, only the date pertaining

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.923
to the index marginalia is noted.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.063
Here's an example.

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:42.420
In the first two full use that the bundle

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:46.160
I've bee working with here on
the left side of the slide,

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:49.760
one finds a list of succinct descriptions,

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:54.090
all numbered, dated, checked
off and all having to do

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:56.930
with how the company attempted
to reduce the presence

00:15:56.930 --> 00:15:58.623
of rotting cloves in the city.

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:04.190
Upon closer inspection, it is
clear that these descriptions

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:07.370
are marginalia selected from the index.

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:10.450
And are at least similar
to if not an exact copy

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:13.374
of the marginalia found

00:16:13.374 --> 00:16:16.074
in the corresponding pages
of the general resolutions.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.870
I'll take the first
marginalia to show exactly

00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:22.070
what the clerk would
have had to do with it.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:27.870
In 1789, a clerk by the
name of Leonard Duriguzzij

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:29.260
must have spent some time

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:32.943
thumbing through the book of
general resolutions from 1703.

00:16:34.862 --> 00:16:37.910
Duriguzzij was one of 300 clerks working

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:39.520
for the company that year.

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:42.530
And his name was included
in the annual name book

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.613
of the company's sworn servants.

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:49.370
Having received instructions
to extract the resolution

00:16:49.370 --> 00:16:52.330
pertaining to the index marginalia,

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:53.570
Duriguzzij what I first scanned

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:56.740
the top of the first 70
pages of the resolutions

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:58.610
table of contents

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:02.283
before finding the page
numbers for the year 1703.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:06.650
Upon finding the month of
June at the top of the page,

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:08.570
he would have scanned it's left margin

00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:10.500
for the specific date.

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:13.040
And upon arriving at the 15th,

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:15.260
he would have seen a
brief list of marginalia

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:16.723
about the day's proceedings.

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:21.110
If he had bothered to peruse these at all,

00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:25.189
he might've learned
that on June 15th, 1703,

00:17:25.189 --> 00:17:27.510
the Tavion administrators
have discussed the shipments

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:31.040
of sun kissed silver and
gold brought into Batavia

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:34.130
from Bengal and the Coromandel coast.

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:37.420
He might've also noticed
that on the same distant day,

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:41.710
a ship loaded with animals
sailed for the city of Palembang

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:44.360
located on Sumatra's east coast,

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:47.003
for the express purpose of hauling pepper.

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:51.450
He would have also that
25,000 pounds of white pepper

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:53.643
were loaded in a vessel bound for Holland.

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:57.820
But as interesting as these
snippets might've been,

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:00.600
Duriguzzij was solely
charged with finding an entry

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:03.180
to the marginalia about cloves.

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:06.000
More specifically, he
was looking for some clue

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:09.507
as to how "the mixing
of moist and dry cloves

00:18:09.507 --> 00:18:11.187
"have been found to be detrimental

00:18:11.187 --> 00:18:13.637
"for this Batavia's climate."

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:19.370
To be sure, Duriguzzij found
that administrators in Batavia

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:21.570
faced a problem with the
cloves that had been stored

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:23.800
in the city's warehouses.

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.580
They had discussed this matter in relation

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:28.560
to the ongoing exports of tree bark

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:31.500
that were to be contrasted
with the demand for spices

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:34.000
from the Western sphere of the East Indies

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:36.573
were Persian and Gujarati
ships remained active.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.090
Having inspected the warehouse stocks,

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:43.330
administrators offered a reflection

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:48.110
as to "the concept of mixing
the moist with the dry cloves,"

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:51.120
deciding that would be
best to separate them,

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:54.110
given the local climate in Batavia.

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:58.100
Moreover, presuming that the
dry ones remained of quality,

00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:00.623
these were to be shipped
separately for sale.

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:05.820
Duriguzzij copied this
passage from the resolutions.

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:09.020
His own copy and extract was then approved

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:11.753
and signed by the head
clerk, Philippas De Elwyk.

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:15.910
Like other company clerks
working under De Elwyk,

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:21.200
Duriguzzij signed his name
on the bottom left corner

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:25.620
of the document innately curling
the first and last letters,

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:27.410
but abbreviating his first name

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:29.533
as what as was common practice.

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:34.600
Now suffice it to say that
these clerks eventually found

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:39.380
that between 1703 and 1719,
administrators in Batavia

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:42.320
tested various labor intensive methods

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:44.790
to rid the city of rotting cloves.

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:48.000
First forcing a group of
people referred in some parts

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:51.360
as are biters workers, and
in other parts of Slava

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:56.360
or slaves to stuff millions
of pounds of rotting cloves

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:58.750
under the city's bulwarks,

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:01.700
to ultimately be moving them years later,

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:03.950
forcing the same group
of people to ship them

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:08.470
to nearby islands and dumping
them in huge man-made holes,

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:09.843
nine feet underground.

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:14.150
This process took around two decades.

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:18.410
In the process of creating
extracts, clerks actively decided

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:20.140
on which passages to include

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:22.500
from the body of the resolutions,

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.100
which always had more information,

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:28.640
more details of methods
and accountings of figures

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:30.323
then were ultimately included.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:35.430
The extracts would be to copies
of more selective fragments,

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:39.230
which would then be weaved
into other company reports.

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:41.843
One genre of which was
called the consideration.

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:50.500
The consideration was one of
many administrative genres

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:54.180
which exuberantly
referenced the paper past.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:58.610
We pleat with fragments of
extracts of past resolutions

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:01.680
using the index, as well as past letters,

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:03.810
contemporary field reports

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:07.240
and handwritten extracts of printed books.

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:10.573
And I did so in order to
argue for new regulations,

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:15.830
as I will show 17th century
expertise would be invoked,

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:19.740
particularly when discussing
matters of the natural world.

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:23.540
It stakes for Imperial commerce
and the necessary labor

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:26.780
required to carry out
the production of spices

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:30.463
in the context of local
colonial governance.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:37.410
In December 22nd, 1775,
the Tavion administrators

00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:40.110
Willem Breton and Hendrick Fockens,

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:42.440
signed their names on the last page

00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:44.693
of a completed consideration.

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:48.820
They had drafted up over
the course of one month,

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:51.520
since having received rain
near the clerks November

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:54.053
field report regarding the
present state of Ambon.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:58.010
The chain of these administrative reports

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:00.710
concerned a problem that had a fraught,

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:03.120
if not an obscure past.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:05.030
The cultivation of nutmeg trees

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:07.653
outside of their native Banda islands.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:12.740
The clerk revealed that in
his report that in fact,

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:15.480
this was not the first time
the company had felt the need

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:18.883
to experiment with nutmeg
cultivation on foreign soil.

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:23.210
Using past letters and best solutions,

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:26.400
he revealed that back in 1695

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:29.810
when a series of devastating
earthquakes disrupted nutmeg

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:32.990
and maize production in
Banda, the high government

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:36.630
had inquired about sustaining
their production elsewhere,

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:39.120
a question of enormous consequence,

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:41.700
considering the company's
century long efforts

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:45.140
to monopolize these very
spices in the Banda islands

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:46.563
for global commerce.

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:50.550
Ultimately after much back and forth,

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:53.490
they settled for the island of Ambon,

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:57.000
reasoning that the Ambonese
people were more experienced

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:00.693
in matters of plant cultivation,
particularly of cloves.

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:06.210
In 1775 Breton and Fockens would learn

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:08.360
from this turn of the century experiment

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:11.150
of nutmeg transplantation.

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:14.660
The specialized knowledge of
preparing nutmeg and maize

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:19.080
from raw fruit parts to
commercially valuable spices

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.700
was not so easily transferable,

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.840
not even among people from
the same subset of islands

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:27.160
in the same region.

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:30.070
The short-lived, but exhaustive experiment

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.633
turned out to be a failure.

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:36.400
By using the clerks field
report, along with extracts

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:40.610
from books, treatises,
correspondences, demonstrations,

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:42.950
and resolutions, Breton and Fockens

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:44.760
would learn from this history

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:48.070
and to make their own judgments
on how best to execute

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:50.170
this delicate operation and their present.

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:56.640
What did they learn from extracts

00:23:56.640 --> 00:23:58.640
of 17th century ethnographic

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:00.813
and natural historical writings was this.

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:06.100
Nutmegs ripe in twice a year in Banda.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:10.120
Their ripeness revealed itself
through the natural splitting

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:13.420
of the nuts wooden shell,
which revealed the mace,

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:17.010
so clear red against the cool black

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:18.743
of the nuts outer layers.

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.820
In the morning, the enslaved
people at the islands

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:26.480
local overseers, who had
direct relations with the VOC

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:29.780
would make their way up the mountain

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:32.500
to the high altitudes
where trees flourished

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:34.990
in the mineral rich soil
surrounding the islands

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:36.593
active volcanoes.

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:40.990
Arriving at the forest, they
would spend their mornings

00:24:40.990 --> 00:24:43.297
and afternoons "plucking."

00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:46.560
Looking up into the sky among the branches

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:48.160
of the nutmeg trees,

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:51.510
they would have seen the red
mace in the crack of the hole,

00:24:51.510 --> 00:24:54.023
and these they knew were
right for the plucking.

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:57.580
They also picked the overripe fruit,

00:24:57.580 --> 00:25:01.510
which had fallen to the ground overnight

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:03.833
and would drop these
into a separate basket.

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:07.430
They knew not to confuse
the fleshly plucked

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:09.450
from the already fallen,

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:12.463
as the two kinds were to be
sold at different prices.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:15.740
Around five in the afternoon,

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:17.950
they walked back down the mountain,

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:20.810
each carrying heavily
loaded baskets of the fruit,

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:23.313
which had to be prepared into spices.

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:27.630
This painstaking work involved, cracking,

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:31.520
unspooling, curing and drying the fruit,

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:34.923
a process which took in
total at least 12 weeks.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:40.070
The extracted passages
detail how in the evenings,

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:42.060
the enslaved sharpen their knives

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.970
to crack the hard, thick,
outer layer of the fruit

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:46.393
completely open.

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:50.620
The cracking proved to
be a difficult process.

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:53.720
They had to apply just the
right amount of pressure

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.950
to cut through the
fruits, shell, and flesh,

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:59.403
but to leave the mace and nut intact.

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:03.010
Based on the abstract, this procedure

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:04.930
was considered dangerous.

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:07.920
Sometimes with a little too much pressure,

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:10.470
the knife would slice all the way through,

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:13.520
which not only left the
nut and mace in pieces,

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:17.537
but also "did away with
the slaves finger or two."

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:20.611
The enslaved also use their
hands to "unspool the red mace

00:26:20.611 --> 00:26:25.611
"from the nut, vein by vein."

00:26:27.080 --> 00:26:30.850
Furthermore, the 17th century
method of drying nutmegs

00:26:30.850 --> 00:26:34.030
required the use of
indigenous technologies,

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:36.300
a complex infrastructure built solely

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:39.290
for applying varying
degrees of heat continuously

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:43.960
for six weeks, then came
the caulking process.

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:47.831
The enslaved would mix salt
water with a type caulk

00:26:47.831 --> 00:26:50.220
of creating a special liquid solution

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:54.323
into which each dried nutmeg
would be dipped three times.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:59.720
The skills, technologies and
knowledge needed to carry out

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:03.130
this long and delicate
process from the plucking

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:05.013
and the cracking,

00:27:05.013 --> 00:27:06.980
to the unspooling, drying and the caulking

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.260
were exactly what rendered the experiment

00:27:10.738 --> 00:27:11.970
of cultivating nutmegs outside of Banda

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:14.507
so "rare and difficult."

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:21.520
As Breton and Fockens learned,
sitting in Batavia in 1775,

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:26.840
back in 1699, administrators
considered bringing to Ambon

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:29.950
a number of "Bandanese overseers"

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:32.573
and a few slaves who understood that work.

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:37.730
The slave say wrote would
be able to impart in time

00:27:37.730 --> 00:27:40.910
the knowledge of how to
handle the trees and fruit

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:43.233
to the indigenous Ambonese people.

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:47.780
But they complained
even after having given

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:49.970
enough courses and lessons,

00:27:49.970 --> 00:27:53.650
the Ambonese seemed to have
lost "the will and diligence"

00:27:53.650 --> 00:27:54.933
to carry out the work.

00:27:56.895 --> 00:27:59.370
They claim that compared
to the slaves from Banda,

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:03.810
the Ambonese were "too
lazy, proud and useless"

00:28:03.810 --> 00:28:05.760
to understand the special treatment

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:08.523
and knowledge necessary
for cultivating nutmegs.

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:12.990
Such turn of the century comparisons

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:16.930
between slaves from Banda
and laborers and Ambon

00:28:16.930 --> 00:28:20.840
influenced Breton and Fockens in 1775,

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:23.790
as they considered how best
to gather enough manpower

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:25.063
for the task at hand.

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:31.200
The two administrators, however
were not entirely convinced

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:34.837
that the Ambonese were
simply "lazy and useless."

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:39.890
After all they argued,
it was the cultivating

00:28:39.890 --> 00:28:41.710
and preparing cloves for the company

00:28:41.710 --> 00:28:43.233
for more than a century.

00:28:44.390 --> 00:28:47.610
They were also unconvinced
of the impossibility

00:28:47.610 --> 00:28:49.670
of imparting the skills
and knowledge needed

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:52.010
to cultivate and prepare nutmegs,

00:28:52.010 --> 00:28:54.313
and what they called the bunnies way.

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:58.720
While they accepted these
impressions and repeated them

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:01.330
in their own report, they began to search

00:29:01.330 --> 00:29:04.890
for an explanation, and
then there are search

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:07.063
they came across the name Assam Soliman,

00:29:07.900 --> 00:29:12.490
whose verbal retort against
the company back in May, 1705

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:16.163
left its own paper trail in
Batavia's provincial archive.

00:29:18.170 --> 00:29:22.150
In 1709, four years after
the recorded incidents

00:29:22.150 --> 00:29:25.150
at the council meeting with
which I began this presentation,

00:29:26.377 --> 00:29:29.100
Joan Van Hoorn included an
extract of the secret letter

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:31.403
from Ambon his own demonstration.

00:29:32.800 --> 00:29:36.100
Van Hoorn's main purpose
for writing in 1709

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:39.830
was to use Soliman as an
example of what he deemed

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:43.460
to be the declining
character of the Ambonese

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:45.370
for which she blamed the company

00:29:45.370 --> 00:29:47.970
and it's focused on commerce.

00:29:47.970 --> 00:29:51.610
It was what he called the
address of company ministers,

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:54.083
which have tainted the
local people with greed.

00:29:55.510 --> 00:29:58.170
Moreover, because of this, he continued,

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:01.620
it would be difficult to
force the to do anything

00:30:01.620 --> 00:30:04.163
without a fence or marked commotion.

00:30:05.890 --> 00:30:10.200
To provide evidence, he cited
Soliman's retort word for word

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:12.337
from the original letter.

00:30:12.337 --> 00:30:14.777
"Are we not now poor enough?

00:30:14.777 --> 00:30:18.097
"What is so hard about letting
them know of our condition?

00:30:18.097 --> 00:30:21.593
"Just let them load the shifts
with ropes to strangle us,

00:30:21.593 --> 00:30:25.207
"and we shall deal with more
of these cloves and then some."

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:29.290
His demonstration would be copied in full

00:30:29.290 --> 00:30:31.660
into the high government's
general resolutions

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:35.590
for the year 1709, and
through its insertion

00:30:35.590 --> 00:30:39.080
into the canon of resolutions,
which clerks consulted

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:41.420
throughout the 18th
century using the index

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:44.920
as I have previously discussed,
it would come to resurface

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:46.950
in Breton and Focken's own report.

00:30:49.439 --> 00:30:51.293
In their search for the
labor needed to cultivate

00:30:51.293 --> 00:30:55.430
nutmegs in Ambon Breton and
Fockens too came came to focus

00:30:55.430 --> 00:30:57.950
on the influence of
commerce on the character

00:30:57.950 --> 00:31:00.380
of the indigenous people.

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:03.220
Echoing Van Hoorn, they
claim that the Ambonese

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:07.610
had left European yearning,
that is the particular ethic

00:31:07.610 --> 00:31:10.863
of merchants whose sole
goal was to make a profit.

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:15.140
Such yearnings, however,
were no longer linked

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:20.040
to notions of greed, but to
the ideal of productivity.

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:23.310
Citing the resolution, which
included Van Hoorns reference

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:26.170
to the secret letter, Breton and Fockens

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:27.993
quoted Soliman's retort,

00:31:28.950 --> 00:31:32.810
and recast this mediated
information from the past,

00:31:32.810 --> 00:31:36.230
in the 1775 report to suit the principles

00:31:36.230 --> 00:31:37.603
of the market economy.

00:31:38.530 --> 00:31:41.470
They recommended that in
order to satisfy the Ambonese

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:44.860
as a workforce, administrators
would have to instill

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:46.093
commercial incentives.

00:31:47.550 --> 00:31:50.180
The clerk had initially
advised the administrators

00:31:50.180 --> 00:31:54.170
to pay the Ambonese
"with a gift of money."

00:31:54.170 --> 00:31:57.217
One that would be "calculated
according to the supply

00:31:57.217 --> 00:31:59.207
"of the previous five years."

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:03.690
Breton and Fockens vehemently disagreed.

00:32:03.690 --> 00:32:07.030
They believed that calculations
based on the previous

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:10.300
five years would not be satisfactory.

00:32:10.300 --> 00:32:13.930
This hinged on another
assumption about how local people

00:32:13.930 --> 00:32:17.700
understood timing and
motivation as they claimed

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:20.307
that "the indigenous sees in the present

00:32:20.307 --> 00:32:21.967
"as much as in the future."

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:26.640
Rather than paying them
based on bundled calculations

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:29.570
from the past then, they
recommended that the company

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:32.673
pay them based on a fixed
amount per weight of production.

00:32:34.010 --> 00:32:36.750
For the two administrators,
the successful cultivation

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:40.780
of nutmegs had everything to
do with instilling motives

00:32:40.780 --> 00:32:43.450
and that transferring
knowledge hinged on increasing

00:32:43.450 --> 00:32:45.343
local peoples incentives.

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:50.000
Such on the ground considerations

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:53.520
and thinking about knowledge,
labor, money and incentives

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:57.430
and controlling the labor
force in the 18th century

00:32:57.430 --> 00:33:00.880
may perhaps explain why at a
time when there was an acute

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:04.450
shortage of VOC coins,
the company would begin

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:07.700
to print paper money, not only in Batavia,

00:33:07.700 --> 00:33:11.810
but also in Ambon in
both Dutch and in Malay

00:33:11.810 --> 00:33:13.923
towards the end of the 18th century.

00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:18.900
The two administrators differed
from their predecessors

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:20.370
in key ways.

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:22.750
While they agreed that 17 send few methods

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:26.720
of preparing the fruit into a
spice was a delicate operation

00:33:26.720 --> 00:33:29.310
of extensive expertise in training,

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:31.260
they disagreed about the impossibility

00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:33.530
of transferring such knowledge.

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:35.560
For them, this was not
a matter of character,

00:33:35.560 --> 00:33:37.950
but a matter of economic motive.

00:33:37.950 --> 00:33:40.100
Furthermore, they were keenly aware

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:43.690
of the company's overwhelming
reliance on Ambonese labor

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:45.543
for the success of their project.

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:50.550
This presentation has dipped
into one or two key moments

00:33:50.550 --> 00:33:53.210
to assess how the emergence
of an 18th century

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:57.200
system of information
retrieval within Batavia

00:33:57.200 --> 00:33:59.530
allowed officials to reach into the past

00:33:59.530 --> 00:34:03.520
in a manner unprecedented to
device on the ground solutions

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:06.000
for specific problems.

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:09.790
It is indeed an intensely
local case of paper chains

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:12.110
from a provincial archive in Batavia,

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:15.400
but also of individuals whose
voices are metaphorically

00:34:15.400 --> 00:34:17.220
chained to the papers,

00:34:17.220 --> 00:34:20.490
which would be replicated
within the system.

00:34:20.490 --> 00:34:23.970
I outlined it was this
process of converting

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:27.040
natural historical and
ethnographic information

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:31.050
into economically and
commercially valuable information,

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:34.291
which would ultimately
inform other reports

00:34:34.291 --> 00:34:37.241
that made their way up the
chain from Batavia to Amsterdam,

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:40.320
as administrators in the provinces,

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:44.711
as well as the metrical
deliberated on how to revive

00:34:44.711 --> 00:34:47.480
past cultivation and
transplantation practices

00:34:47.480 --> 00:34:49.093
for their present dilemmas.

00:34:50.530 --> 00:34:53.050
In effect, these
administrators became producers

00:34:53.050 --> 00:34:55.310
and reproducers of information

00:34:55.310 --> 00:34:58.310
that fed an ever-growing paper bureaucracy

00:34:58.310 --> 00:35:02.228
lending the last to accompany,
which was by the last half

00:35:02.228 --> 00:35:03.950
of the 18th century, drowning in debt

00:35:03.950 --> 00:35:05.910
and crippled by interim material wars

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:07.463
across the Indian ocean.

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:11.911
During this process, information contained

00:35:11.911 --> 00:35:13.590
within various administrative writings

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:17.910
came to have a protracted
lifespan as they were recycled

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:20.423
and extracts over the
course of the century.

00:35:21.580 --> 00:35:24.380
I hope this shows the power
of provincial archives

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:28.532
for historical actors
'cause zone prognostications

00:35:28.532 --> 00:35:30.250
were based on fragments
of mediated information

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:31.510
from a different time.

00:35:31.510 --> 00:35:32.343
Thank you.

00:35:37.150 --> 00:35:38.653
- Thank you so much, Genie.

00:35:40.484 --> 00:35:43.320
Let's take a moment everyone
to reflect on this talk

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:46.093
for a moment while you
come up with questions.

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:51.010
We'll start the Q&amp;A in just a moment or so

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:52.873
as people gather their thoughts.

00:35:54.600 --> 00:35:56.870
That was a very rich and
wonderful presentation.

00:35:56.870 --> 00:35:57.973
Thank you very much.

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:03.570
To the guests here, I
will moderate the Q&amp;A.

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:07.310
If you have a question, please
use the hand raise function,

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:09.950
or turn your video on
and wave your hand wildly

00:36:09.950 --> 00:36:11.900
and I'll probably see you that way too.

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:17.960
Michael, I see that you have
you've turned your video on.

00:36:17.960 --> 00:36:20.883
Does that indicate an
interest in asking a question?

00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:26.083
- Absolutely.

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:30.670
Thank you.

00:36:30.670 --> 00:36:32.423
This fascinating talk.

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:38.363
You have published on Rumphius
who was one of the great

00:36:41.440 --> 00:36:46.440
encyclopedic compilers of
information of the 17th century.

00:36:49.760 --> 00:36:51.600
So, listening to you talk,

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:56.600
I'm wondering if there's any evidence

00:37:00.139 --> 00:37:01.973
that some of these VOC bureaucrats

00:37:07.243 --> 00:37:12.037
and archivist referenced
either any of his methods

00:37:15.450 --> 00:37:20.450
or his reports to Batavia
or in the 18th century,

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:28.810
his publications, because of
course on spice production,

00:37:31.360 --> 00:37:36.360
he was for a time probably
the world's expert

00:37:38.770 --> 00:37:40.593
for that part of the world.

00:37:43.510 --> 00:37:46.377
- Yeah, thank you so much,
Michael, for the question.

00:37:48.171 --> 00:37:52.937
I have indirect evidence that
VOC administrators in Batavia

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:58.470
were using information
which Rumphius had collected

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:03.343
back in the 17th century,
mainly through their use

00:38:05.550 --> 00:38:10.550
of Francois Valentines,
all the new East Indies.

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:13.560
And as we know, Valentine
was intimately connected

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:18.560
to Rumphius and derived
a lot of his own writings

00:38:19.900 --> 00:38:23.733
about spices from Rumphius' works.

00:38:25.834 --> 00:38:29.970
Rumphius' natural history
as herbal wasn't published

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:33.833
until the middle of the
18th century in Holland.

00:38:37.945 --> 00:38:40.867
The print itself wasn't
as famous until the 19th

00:38:40.867 --> 00:38:43.850
and 20th centuries when
it would we circulate

00:38:43.850 --> 00:38:48.850
back to the islands to influence renewed

00:38:49.880 --> 00:38:52.963
by a prospecting activities and Ambon.

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:59.430
But for the 18th century, I
don't have direct evidence

00:38:59.430 --> 00:39:04.163
of VOC clerks in Batavia
using Rumphius' works.

00:39:05.470 --> 00:39:08.240
They mainly site Valentine who at the time

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:12.643
is much more famous for
his printed ethnography.

00:39:15.286 --> 00:39:18.710
But I wouldn't be surprised
if they were influenced

00:39:18.710 --> 00:39:22.490
by not Rumphius' natural histories,

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:27.490
but as administrative papers,
many of which he dictated

00:39:28.670 --> 00:39:32.440
and his scribe wrote down, and
many of which had a signature

00:39:33.910 --> 00:39:35.823
at the end of the document.

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:41.760
So there was probably some
influence from Rumphius,

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:46.760
but not so much in the
18th century as I can see.

00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:51.513
Mainly in the 19th and the 20th centuries,

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:56.190
as well as the 17th century
when he was reporting back

00:39:56.190 --> 00:39:59.143
to Batavia constantly.

00:40:01.430 --> 00:40:02.263
- Thank you.

00:40:04.410 --> 00:40:07.773
- While people collect their
thoughts, I have a question.

00:40:10.469 --> 00:40:11.610
As you were giving your talk,

00:40:11.610 --> 00:40:14.030
I couldn't help, but think to myself

00:40:15.209 --> 00:40:19.753
that your one more addition
to the paper chain in a sense.

00:40:20.590 --> 00:40:24.560
You're writing a
dissertation about the way

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:28.610
in which people are
referring to other texts

00:40:28.610 --> 00:40:30.900
and the text appear and they reappear.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:33.070
And in a sense, they reappear

00:40:33.070 --> 00:40:35.360
on your PowerPoint here as well.

00:40:35.360 --> 00:40:40.360
And so I wondered if you
could tell us the difference

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:44.010
and why does this period matter

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:46.810
and why does this place matter?

00:40:46.810 --> 00:40:50.190
Specifically, so if you're
doing, in some sense,

00:40:50.190 --> 00:40:52.830
you could argue that you're
doing the same thing,

00:40:52.830 --> 00:40:55.840
but you are not in the same place in time.

00:40:55.840 --> 00:41:00.800
And so does the meaning
change of the process,

00:41:00.800 --> 00:41:03.620
or is it a kind of
universal historical process

00:41:03.620 --> 00:41:07.500
that you're hinting at, but
then takes on a specific meaning

00:41:07.500 --> 00:41:09.900
in a particular place in time?

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:12.660
And so I'm wondering if
you could then reflect

00:41:12.660 --> 00:41:16.690
on how this is specific in
a way to the 18th century

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:21.070
and to Dutch colonialism in this region

00:41:21.070 --> 00:41:24.270
and then how that differentiates it

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:26.740
to being a Princeton
scholar writing about it

00:41:26.740 --> 00:41:28.543
in the 21st century.

00:41:29.850 --> 00:41:32.100
- No, Erik, thank you so
much for that question.

00:41:32.100 --> 00:41:35.730
Yeah, it's strange kind
of take a step back

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:39.230
from the dissertation writing
and reflect on the fact

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:43.330
that this kind of paper chain continues

00:41:43.330 --> 00:41:44.863
upto the present day.

00:41:46.427 --> 00:41:48.730
And of course there are clear parallels

00:41:48.730 --> 00:41:51.960
between the way that these VOC clerics

00:41:53.861 --> 00:41:56.880
and administrators governing an empire

00:41:56.880 --> 00:42:01.880
use the archive to write
about these islands,

00:42:02.090 --> 00:42:04.783
not only ethnographically,
but historically,

00:42:06.834 --> 00:42:08.563
and for policy and regulation purposes.

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:15.120
I think I see this process happening

00:42:15.120 --> 00:42:17.770
in multiple ways and I'll come back

00:42:17.770 --> 00:42:22.130
to how I think of my role in all of this.

00:42:22.130 --> 00:42:26.780
The latest translator, for
example of Rumphius' verbal,

00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:34.160
Ian Batemon, I recently found out

00:42:34.164 --> 00:42:36.900
was also kind of collaborating
with a pharmaceutical company

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:37.733
in the U.S.

00:42:38.720 --> 00:42:43.170
And so like every kind
of mediating process,

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:48.043
whether that's collecting,
documenting, translating,

00:42:49.387 --> 00:42:51.987
circulating similar information
and various versions

00:42:53.009 --> 00:42:55.820
has these on the ground and impacts

00:42:55.820 --> 00:43:00.820
that I hope to kind of go into
the present day for the book.

00:43:03.890 --> 00:43:08.890
But I think my role is to
kind of focus on the history

00:43:14.870 --> 00:43:18.670
of mediation and its influence

00:43:18.670 --> 00:43:23.120
in order to make this a
little more as historians,

00:43:23.120 --> 00:43:28.120
self-reflexive about the craft we practice

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:33.680
and how every kind of historical
document that we're reading

00:43:34.200 --> 00:43:39.200
is buttressed by all kinds
of mediating influences

00:43:40.270 --> 00:43:42.383
and elements of uncertainty.

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:48.960
So it's certainly been
humbling, but at the same time,

00:43:48.960 --> 00:43:53.960
I am very much aware that I
too am repeating their words

00:43:56.050 --> 00:44:01.050
and I guess it's just
one way of exploring,

00:44:05.380 --> 00:44:10.380
but also critiquing the
way we narrate the past.

00:44:10.760 --> 00:44:13.633
So yeah.

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:18.660
- Thank you for this,
it's very interesting.

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:20.480
There's a lot to think about there.

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:22.220
There's a question, I don't
know if you can see it

00:44:22.220 --> 00:44:23.053
in the chat.

00:44:23.965 --> 00:44:26.603
Can you see the chat window
