was spoken by Soliman or
that you had questions

00:48:19.670 --> 00:48:22.540
about its authenticity, can you say more?

00:48:22.540 --> 00:48:26.050
And I'm wondering if he was
specifically being recited

00:48:26.050 --> 00:48:29.240
and held up as some kind of "genuine"

00:48:29.240 --> 00:48:31.373
or authentic local voice.

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:38.677
- Yeah, I had someone ask
me this question previously

00:48:39.800 --> 00:48:42.740
in a different setting,

00:48:42.740 --> 00:48:46.570
and it's really unclear
to me how it's possible

00:48:46.570 --> 00:48:51.570
to kind of site Soliman
word for word in Dutch,

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:57.200
mainly because we also
don't know what language

00:48:57.200 --> 00:48:59.870
they were speaking in
at the council meetings.

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:02.760
So I'm not sure that it's a translation

00:49:02.760 --> 00:49:07.410
and also just substantively,
it didn't make sense

00:49:07.410 --> 00:49:11.200
in the context that they were in,

00:49:11.200 --> 00:49:16.200
that the company was asking
to reduce the number of cloves

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:21.200
and yet Soliman's retort
suggests that he was being asked

00:49:22.560 --> 00:49:24.443
to deal with more cloves.

00:49:25.856 --> 00:49:30.856
And so that's certainly an
question and I hope it was clear

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:36.120
that, like, I also think I
have my doubts about this.

00:49:36.120 --> 00:49:41.120
But the important thing is
that for the administrators

00:49:41.333 --> 00:49:45.183
who were working with it in this system,

00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:48.530
it didn't really matter
whether they thought

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:49.670
it was true or not.

00:49:49.670 --> 00:49:54.670
They could provide evidence
by sending somebody as witness

00:49:56.210 --> 00:50:01.207
to the event to Batavia
and testify in person.

00:50:03.476 --> 00:50:06.840
And of course, the
clerks and administrators

00:50:06.840 --> 00:50:09.310
were so incredibly meticulous to a point

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:13.750
where as I'm tracing his
words in multiple documents

00:50:13.750 --> 00:50:18.290
across the century,
there is no misspelling

00:50:18.290 --> 00:50:20.950
or changing of the actual quote.

00:50:20.950 --> 00:50:23.920
I mean, they're incredibly
meticulous in writing it down

00:50:23.920 --> 00:50:27.670
in the same way, it's
just that interpreter

00:50:28.527 --> 00:50:30.160
as if it's kind of frozen in time.

00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:33.420
And then the interpretations
around the quote

00:50:33.420 --> 00:50:35.093
changes over time.

00:50:40.550 --> 00:50:45.550
Yeah, I mean, they're citing
local indigenous people

00:50:46.857 --> 00:50:47.690
all the time.

00:50:47.690 --> 00:50:49.023
This is not something that's unique.

00:50:51.430 --> 00:50:56.430
So I don't really think
that they were being recited

00:51:00.720 --> 00:51:05.103
as kind of an authentic indigenous voice,

00:51:06.080 --> 00:51:10.540
but more as evidence
or different arguments

00:51:10.540 --> 00:51:12.260
at the time that they were writing.

00:51:12.260 --> 00:51:17.020
So of course, in 1709 as Van
Hoorn was citing Soliman,

00:51:18.360 --> 00:51:22.970
he's using it to argue that the company

00:51:22.970 --> 00:51:27.970
had somehow tainted
local people's character.

00:51:29.910 --> 00:51:33.363
And it doesn't have much
to do with authenticity,

00:51:34.890 --> 00:51:38.410
but that here is this evidence
that this Ambonese person

00:51:38.410 --> 00:51:41.453
has changed, he is not compliant.

00:51:44.575 --> 00:51:49.575
And of course by 1775,
they're citing him again,

00:51:49.770 --> 00:51:52.660
not to say that the company administrators

00:51:52.660 --> 00:51:57.290
tainted this man's
character, but out of fear

00:51:58.480 --> 00:52:03.033
that people are not gonna be
happy with just a fixed price

00:52:05.960 --> 00:52:10.590
based on bundled
calculations from the cost

00:52:12.368 --> 00:52:13.600
and the cost of probes
previous five years.

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:18.600
So it's kind of an evidence

00:52:19.610 --> 00:52:23.920
for how they think the
indigenous people will react

00:52:23.920 --> 00:52:28.840
to certain policies, but I don't think

00:52:28.840 --> 00:52:31.150
it's just about this kind of unique,

00:52:31.150 --> 00:52:33.663
authentic indigenous voice.

00:52:36.580 --> 00:52:38.780
- Thank you, we have a
question from Al Lim.

00:52:40.410 --> 00:52:43.230
- Hi, thank you so much
for your presentation.

00:52:43.230 --> 00:52:45.220
I'm not a historian, but I'm just curious

00:52:45.220 --> 00:52:48.290
about kind of the
correlations coming a bit more

00:52:49.262 --> 00:52:50.620
from like an anthropological side,

00:52:50.620 --> 00:52:53.760
specifically with Batavia
and the kind of relationship

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:55.020
on the colonial relationship.

00:52:55.020 --> 00:52:58.477
So you're talking about the
governor who wrote the letter

00:52:58.477 --> 00:52:59.310
to the Batavia.

00:52:59.310 --> 00:53:00.670
I'm wondering kind of, if you
could speak a little more,

00:53:00.670 --> 00:53:04.450
who is on the receiving end
and thinking about how they,

00:53:04.450 --> 00:53:06.460
and in the top...

00:53:06.460 --> 00:53:08.470
Right up, you talked
about how there was a need

00:53:08.470 --> 00:53:10.890
to resolve the present
dilemmas for what they believe

00:53:10.890 --> 00:53:13.490
to be a better future and
this kind of paper chain.

00:53:13.490 --> 00:53:15.980
So I'm wondering who is the one
kind of shaping this future?

00:53:15.980 --> 00:53:17.963
What future did they kind of imagine,

00:53:19.553 --> 00:53:21.810
and kind of like who was the one deciding

00:53:21.810 --> 00:53:25.530
that they need to convert
this natural history knowledge

00:53:25.530 --> 00:53:28.390
into an economically valuable one

00:53:28.390 --> 00:53:31.240
and kind of like who is on the other side?

00:53:31.240 --> 00:53:32.073
Thank you.

00:53:33.810 --> 00:53:36.933
- Yeah, thank you so
much for that question.

00:53:36.933 --> 00:53:41.240
There's kind of a clear
hierarchy within the company.

00:53:41.240 --> 00:53:44.490
And of course, administrators
stationed in Batavia

00:53:44.490 --> 00:53:47.740
are much more powerful than administrators

00:53:47.740 --> 00:53:51.320
stationed in these other
port cities like Ambon.

00:53:51.320 --> 00:53:54.320
Ambon actually becomes
this weird training ground

00:53:57.630 --> 00:54:01.130
to get a position as a
high ranking administrator

00:54:01.130 --> 00:54:02.483
in Batavia.

00:54:04.070 --> 00:54:08.750
But of course, within the kind
of European company sphere,

00:54:08.750 --> 00:54:12.720
the clerks, the marginalists
and the archivists

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:17.720
are also not, I mean, they're
kind of low in the hierarchy.

00:54:19.320 --> 00:54:21.080
We don't really hear their voices,

00:54:21.080 --> 00:54:23.523
all we have or their signatures.

00:54:24.640 --> 00:54:26.580
But they were part of the system

00:54:28.670 --> 00:54:33.670
of using the archive to
basically collect information

00:54:34.250 --> 00:54:37.890
from the past for forming
present resolutions.

00:54:37.890 --> 00:54:41.780
And of course, much of this
is going on in Batavia,

00:54:41.780 --> 00:54:46.780
but this paper chain moves all
the way from outer provinces,

00:54:48.520 --> 00:54:52.540
like in outer provinces
in Ambon to Batavia

00:54:52.540 --> 00:54:54.683
and from Batavia to Amsterdam.

00:54:55.940 --> 00:54:59.150
So ultimately all of these
reports are being written

00:55:00.350 --> 00:55:03.800
for the governor general in Batavia,

00:55:03.800 --> 00:55:07.080
but also those in Amsterdam
who are much more powerful

00:55:07.080 --> 00:55:10.093
than anyone stationed
in these port cities.

00:55:12.340 --> 00:55:17.340
And of course like the
people who are the least

00:55:18.030 --> 00:55:22.080
or just have no power within this process,

00:55:22.080 --> 00:55:25.523
I would say are kind of
people like Assam Soliman,

00:55:26.872 --> 00:55:31.872
whose voices we think we
hear, but we're left in doubt

00:55:32.750 --> 00:55:36.670
as to whether that was
actually what he said,

00:55:36.670 --> 00:55:40.203
whether he's being
interpreted in the right way.

00:55:41.310 --> 00:55:45.600
And of course, even when
I talk about the knowledge

00:55:45.600 --> 00:55:50.540
of slaves from Banda,
it's not as if we have

00:55:57.470 --> 00:56:00.434
their voices in the sense.

00:56:00.434 --> 00:56:03.673
It's much more about really
collecting information,

00:56:09.149 --> 00:56:13.149
rather than kind of in
any way, trying to revive

00:56:17.330 --> 00:56:20.270
their agency within these documents.

00:56:20.270 --> 00:56:23.210
And I'm a bit wary of doing that

00:56:23.210 --> 00:56:27.600
mainly because it's kind
of like, who am I to do it?

00:56:27.600 --> 00:56:32.047
But also it's interesting
to see that by looking

00:56:33.440 --> 00:56:36.820
at the paper chains, one realizes that...

00:56:36.820 --> 00:56:41.820
I mean, I think I could
have written this chapter

00:56:41.990 --> 00:56:45.690
on this paper just solely
focusing on Assam Soliman

00:56:45.690 --> 00:56:47.660
about whom I have a
little more information,

00:56:47.660 --> 00:56:50.040
which I didn't present today.

00:56:50.040 --> 00:56:54.900
But ultimately it became a
case of how the structure,

00:56:54.900 --> 00:56:59.700
which I've outlined here of
information being recycled

00:56:59.700 --> 00:57:04.080
in the archive completely
overpowering any kind of voice

00:57:04.080 --> 00:57:07.670
I can trace, which I've
tried really hard to do.

00:57:07.670 --> 00:57:10.403
So thank you for your question on power.

00:57:12.493 --> 00:57:15.910
It's everywhere and also kind of nowhere.

00:57:15.910 --> 00:57:17.343
So thank you.

00:57:24.750 --> 00:57:25.880
- I don't see any other questions,

00:57:25.880 --> 00:57:28.370
but I'm gonna ask one last question.

00:57:28.370 --> 00:57:30.340
It's kind of a theoretical question,

00:57:30.340 --> 00:57:33.770
but you mentioned the word
agency in the documents

00:57:33.770 --> 00:57:36.660
and you're a little wary about that.

00:57:36.660 --> 00:57:40.050
And I was pleasantly
surprised in your talk,

00:57:40.050 --> 00:57:42.920
how you didn't go down the pathway

00:57:42.920 --> 00:57:44.970
that's almost predictable nowadays

00:57:44.970 --> 00:57:48.020
when people talk about
documents is in the agency

00:57:48.020 --> 00:57:50.160
of the non-human of the document

00:57:50.160 --> 00:57:51.783
and all of these kinds of things,

00:57:53.451 --> 00:57:56.610
which is interesting
theoretically in many ways.

00:57:56.610 --> 00:58:01.210
But sometimes it departs
from the actual material

00:58:01.210 --> 00:58:04.370
so much that you kind of
lose the story a little bit,

00:58:04.370 --> 00:58:05.940
but it does raise a question for me.

00:58:05.940 --> 00:58:07.650
I'm curious like what you think

00:58:07.650 --> 00:58:12.150
about the kind of literature
and things going on

00:58:12.150 --> 00:58:14.580
about the agency of
documents and all that.

00:58:14.580 --> 00:58:16.690
You've certainly, you must be
thinking about some of that

00:58:16.690 --> 00:58:19.800
a little bit as you write,
but you have a unique approach

00:58:19.800 --> 00:58:20.633
to it, it seems.

00:58:20.633 --> 00:58:22.870
I wonder if you could articulate the way

00:58:22.870 --> 00:58:24.320
you think about those things.

00:58:26.170 --> 00:58:29.543
- Yeah, no, thank you so
much for this question.

00:58:30.990 --> 00:58:33.135
It is something I'm considering,

00:58:33.135 --> 00:58:35.900
but ultimately I think my
whole project is kind of based

00:58:35.900 --> 00:58:40.900
on the power of reading
and the power of reading

00:58:42.440 --> 00:58:46.950
and interpreting and changing
value and the information

00:58:46.950 --> 00:58:50.223
that people have collected
and read in the past,

00:58:51.230 --> 00:58:56.230
which kind of goes into my
writing about mediation,

00:58:57.260 --> 00:58:59.993
that these documents don't...

00:59:05.453 --> 00:59:10.440
I think that it's really the
people who are kind of behind

00:59:10.440 --> 00:59:13.310
the documents who have the most agency

00:59:13.310 --> 00:59:15.900
and yet are not exactly talked about,

00:59:15.900 --> 00:59:19.517
and particularly in
the context of the VOC.

00:59:24.353 --> 00:59:29.353
So, yeah, I haven't really
mentioned the agency

00:59:34.114 --> 00:59:38.850
of archival documents so much
as the agency of the clerks

00:59:38.850 --> 00:59:43.850
and the archivists who are
actively compiling information

00:59:44.790 --> 00:59:47.113
and organizing that information.

00:59:48.770 --> 00:59:50.250
And of course, along the way,

00:59:50.250 --> 00:59:54.730
the transformation that
happens as they reinterpret

00:59:54.730 --> 00:59:59.270
or selectively extract shorter passages

00:59:59.270 --> 01:00:01.023
from longer resolutions.

01:00:02.895 --> 01:00:05.345
There are decisions being
made that I don't think

01:00:06.672 --> 01:00:07.760
that historians have fully
considered when they read

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:10.420
their historical documents.

01:00:10.420 --> 01:00:15.420
For example, the first
part of my presentation,

01:00:18.380 --> 01:00:22.400
the documents have the
date of the extracts,

01:00:22.400 --> 01:00:25.913
which would be from like 1703 to 1715,

01:00:26.920 --> 01:00:30.300
but they don't have the
date for when those extracts

01:00:30.300 --> 01:00:32.130
were collected.

01:00:32.130 --> 01:00:36.360
One would have to figure
out the names of the clerks

01:00:36.360 --> 01:00:38.870
and then trace the names of the clerks

01:00:38.870 --> 01:00:42.900
in the VOC little name
books in order to figure out

01:00:42.900 --> 01:00:45.713
when they were extracted
and for what purposes.

01:00:47.610 --> 01:00:50.430
And so I think that's a good
case of showing that like

01:00:54.200 --> 01:00:57.550
a lot of these documents
have been mediated

01:00:57.550 --> 01:00:59.890
well for a very long period of time.

01:00:59.890 --> 01:01:01.770
And even within a very
short period of time,

01:01:01.770 --> 01:01:04.140
if I talk about Rumphius,
all of those documents

01:01:04.140 --> 01:01:05.930
have been mediated in certain ways,

01:01:05.930 --> 01:01:10.930
whether it's changing indigenous
knowledge or using a scribe

01:01:11.610 --> 01:01:15.920
to dictating and using a
scribe to document certain

01:01:15.920 --> 01:01:20.420
information, copies of copies of copies,

01:01:20.420 --> 01:01:22.690
many of which are very different.

01:01:22.690 --> 01:01:27.690
And so I'm not sure that
the object has as much power

01:01:32.257 --> 01:01:35.030
as I would like to imbue them.

01:01:35.030 --> 01:01:37.960
I think it's really the people
who are kind of invisible

01:01:37.960 --> 01:01:39.590
or have not been talked about

01:01:39.590 --> 01:01:43.470
where for a much more powerful.

01:01:43.470 --> 01:01:45.770
And I try to be honest about that.

01:01:45.770 --> 01:01:46.603
Yeah.

01:01:48.254 --> 01:01:49.087
- It's interesting.

01:01:49.087 --> 01:01:51.060
I was thinking a little bit
about the system itself,

01:01:51.060 --> 01:01:55.090
having a kind of compulsion
to it as well, though,

01:01:55.090 --> 01:01:58.950
for example, when you mentioned
that very important shift

01:01:58.950 --> 01:02:02.820
from listing by date and discovering

01:02:02.820 --> 01:02:05.020
that that conceals things or something,

01:02:05.020 --> 01:02:08.060
and then does the desire to change it

01:02:08.060 --> 01:02:09.660
to an alphabetical system, right?

01:02:09.660 --> 01:02:12.490
But once you shift to
an alphabetical system,

01:02:12.490 --> 01:02:15.110
you have to fill in a through Z, right.

01:02:15.110 --> 01:02:19.780
And eventually that itself
generates a certain kind process,

01:02:20.890 --> 01:02:23.290
say, you've finished A,
well, then you're not done,

01:02:23.290 --> 01:02:24.920
you know you have to do B

01:02:24.920 --> 01:02:29.080
and it creates a kind of
like compulsion within itself

01:02:29.080 --> 01:02:31.000
that is quite interesting.

01:02:31.000 --> 01:02:34.350
But the initial process is
started by a desire to shift.

01:02:34.350 --> 01:02:36.440
So it's quite interesting.

01:02:36.440 --> 01:02:38.290
- Yeah, it's kind of...

01:02:38.290 --> 01:02:40.900
I found it really interesting
that they didn't have

01:02:40.900 --> 01:02:44.590
the system in the 17th
century that at something

01:02:44.590 --> 01:02:48.400
that started very much
in the 18th century,

01:02:48.400 --> 01:02:51.570
the shift from, well,
it's not a complete shift

01:02:51.570 --> 01:02:55.230
because these resolutions are
still being written by date.

01:02:55.230 --> 01:02:57.910
It's just that now they
have this massive index

01:02:58.909 --> 01:03:01.703
to be able to organize that information.

01:03:03.950 --> 01:03:08.950
But yeah, it's certainly
kind of the way of thinking.

01:03:09.750 --> 01:03:14.450
And there isn't just one index,
this is kind of a process

01:03:17.350 --> 01:03:20.840
that spans most of the 18th century.

01:03:20.840 --> 01:03:23.950
So there are multiple registers,

01:03:23.950 --> 01:03:27.280
which are then later compiled
and printed in the 19th

01:03:28.647 --> 01:03:30.173
and 20th centuries.

01:03:33.290 --> 01:03:36.830
And you know, in the 19th
century print edition,

01:03:36.830 --> 01:03:39.367
they will say, "Oh, this is for students

01:03:39.367 --> 01:03:44.367
"who might be curious about
the history of the VOC."

01:03:44.410 --> 01:03:48.950
So students can actually
use the index to search for,

01:03:48.950 --> 01:03:50.833
I guess, thesis topics or something.

01:03:52.180 --> 01:03:54.370
And just to see if there
are enough documents

01:03:54.370 --> 01:03:55.520
about that one topic.

01:03:55.520 --> 01:03:59.450
So it is definitely kind of a major shift

01:03:59.450 --> 01:04:04.450
in thinking about how to
research the VOC past.

01:04:08.438 --> 01:04:09.630
- I think if there's no further questions,

01:04:09.630 --> 01:04:12.290
I'm gonna bring the talk to a close.

01:04:12.290 --> 01:04:13.660
So I'll stop the recording now

01:04:13.660 --> 01:04:16.350
and I'll leave the window
open for a few more minutes

01:04:16.350 --> 01:04:18.190
for informal conversation after that,

01:04:18.190 --> 01:04:22.470
but please everyone join
me in thanking Genie Yoo

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:25.030
for such a fascinating
talk and wonderful Q&amp;A.

01:04:25.030 --> 01:04:26.233
So thank you.

